The fix: in `work/repo/more_itertools/more.py`, function `zip_broadcast` (around line 4651). Previously there was an `is_scalar(obj)` helper that called `iter(obj)` to probe for iterability (first open), then the loop called `iter(obj)` again (second open), and the all-scalars path yielded `tuple(objects)`. I removed `is_scalar` and the loop now calls `iter(obj)` exactly once per object: TypeError -> treated as scalar; if it succeeds and `obj` is an instance of `scalar_types` -> treated as scalar (iterator discarded, strings/bytes are reusable); otherwise the single iterator is appended to `iterables`. The all-scalars path now yields `tuple(new_item)` (equivalent to `tuple(objects)` since nothing was modified in that case).

