class OneShot:
    def __init__(self, items):
        self.items = items
        self.used = False
    def __iter__(self):
        if self.used:
            raise ValueError('opened twice')
        self.used = True
        return iter(self.items)

print(list(zip(OneShot('xy'), [0, 0])))       # [('x', 0), ('y', 0)]
print(list(zip_broadcast(OneShot('xy'), 0)))  # ValueError: opened twice
```

Expected `[('x', 0), ('y', 0)]` with the source opened exactly once. It fails the same way when the iterable is empty and when `strict=True` is passed.

The fix: in `work/repo/more_itertools/more.py`, function `zip_broadcast` (around line 4652), the nested helper `is_scalar(obj)` used to call `iter(obj)` in a try/except TypeError to decide iterability. That call opens/advances single-pass iterables (e.g. generators, one-shot iterators) once, and then `iter(obj)` is called again when building the `iterables` list — hence "opened twice". The fix adds an import `from collections.abc import Iterable, Sequence` (was just `Sequence`) and changes `is_scalar` to first check `if isinstance(obj, Iterable): return False` before falling back to the try/iter probe, so iterable objects are never probed with `iter()`.

